{
  "image_name": "1f1081b33cece071ac1b4681a3d5b792.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified lineage map of blood and immune cell development that highlights where specific long non-coding RNAs act during differentiation. It starts with hematopoietic stem cells progressing through short-term stem cells and multipotent progenitors, then splitting into myeloid and lymphoid branches.\n\nOn the myeloid side, common myeloid progenitors give rise to granulocyte–monocyte progenitors and then to monocytes, eosinophils, neutrophils, and dendritic cells, with labels indicating RNA regulators such as HOTAIRM1, MORRBID, Linc-EPS, and lnc-DC. Monocytes further differentiate into macrophages. On the lymphoid side, common lymphoid progenitors generate B cells, CD4+ T cells, CD8+ T cells, and innate lymphoid cell precursors, which then form ILC1, ILC2, and ILC3.\n\nThe main message is that distinct non-coding RNAs are associated with key branch points or mature immune subsets. Examples include H19 and XIST near early stem cells, NRON in CD4+ T cells, linc-MAF4 and a CCR2-related antisense RNA around T-helper cell fates, FLICR in regulatory T cells, and IFNG-AS1 near both TH1 and cytotoxic T cell interferon-γ production. Overall, the image emphasizes RNA-based control of immune lineage specification and function.",
